# The rig, assembled: deck, six piles, the tide seam, and all fourteen
# bulwark rail/cap runs, in one scene with one gate row watching it.
#
# Tasks 3b, 4 and 5 each replaced one `deeper_demo.loom` primitive stand-in
# with textured mesh(es) and a transcribed collider, proved node by node in
# their own scenes (`rig_deck.loom`, and the substructure/bulwark equivalents
# this task's brief points at). This scene is the first place all four zones
# — deck, piles, seam, bulwark — share a frame, so it is the first place
# their materials can be judged as one structure rather than four isolated
# swatches.
#
# **Every position and half-extent below is copied from a generator's stdout,
# not retyped from the plan.** `build_substructure.py` prints the pile's local
# mesh extent and the seam's world range / node_y / `BoxCollider` — rerun
# immediately before authoring this file:
#
#     pile: 60 tris, x -0.350..0.350 y -1.200..1.200 z -0.350..0.350
#     seam: 60 tris, world y -0.650..0.450, node_y=-0.100,
#           collider half_extents=[0.350, 0.550, 0.350], straddles the waterline
#
# `build_bulwark.py` prints all fourteen rail/cap node positions and
# `BoxCollider` half-extents in one table; every `Rail*`/`Cap*` node below is
# one row of that table, unedited:
#
#   rail South     node_pos=(  0.000, 1.900,   6.900) half_extents=(12.000, 0.500,  0.100)
#   rail West      node_pos=(-11.900, 1.900,   0.000) half_extents=( 0.100, 0.500,  7.000)
#   rail EastNorth node_pos=( 11.900, 1.900,  -2.500) half_extents=( 0.100, 0.500,  4.500)
#   rail EastSouth node_pos=( 11.900, 1.900,   5.900) half_extents=( 0.100, 0.500,  1.100)
#   rail NorthWest node_pos=(-11.150, 1.900,  -6.900) half_extents=( 0.850, 0.500,  0.100)
#   rail NorthMid  node_pos=( -7.600, 1.900,  -6.900) half_extents=( 0.700, 0.500,  0.100)
#   rail NorthEast node_pos=(  4.450, 1.900,  -6.900) half_extents=( 7.550, 0.500,  0.100)
#   cap  South     node_pos=(  0.000, 2.800,   6.900) half_extents=(12.000, 0.120,  0.060)
#   cap  NorthWest node_pos=(-11.150, 2.800,  -6.900) half_extents=( 0.850, 0.120,  0.060)
#   cap  NorthMid  node_pos=( -7.600, 2.800,  -6.900) half_extents=( 0.700, 0.120,  0.060)
#   cap  NorthEast node_pos=(  4.450, 2.800,  -6.900) half_extents=( 7.550, 0.120,  0.060)
#   cap  West      node_pos=(-11.900, 2.800,   0.000) half_extents=( 0.060, 0.120,  7.000)
#   cap  EastNorth node_pos=( 11.900, 2.800,  -2.500) half_extents=( 0.060, 0.120,  4.500)
#   cap  EastSouth node_pos=( 11.900, 2.800,   5.900) half_extents=( 0.060, 0.120,  1.100)
#
# Pile/Seam x,z are the generator's own constants (`PILE_X = (-10.5, 0.0,
# 10.5)`, `PILE_Z = (-5.5, 5.5)`), and the six positions below are those two
# sets crossed, at y = -0.20 (pile) / y = -0.10 (seam) — the node heights that
# put a LOCAL-CENTRED mesh at its authored world height, same story as
# `rig_deck.loom`'s header on `Deck`. Every one of these numbers also matches
# `deeper_demo.loom`'s own primitive stand-ins bit for bit (PilingNW..SE,
# RailSouth..NorthEast, CapSouth..EastSouth) — this scene is a drop-in
# replacement at unchanged transforms, which is what Phase 4 needs it to be.
#
# **The bracing is deliberately NOT in this scene.** `rig_bracing.obj` exists
# and is built, but Task 4 measured what wiring it costs: a `BoxCollider`
# matching its true AABB is a 21 x 11 m slab (the bracing is an open lattice
# of eight thin rods and a box cannot express a lattice), and omitting the
# component instead gives it a 2x2x2 m cube at the rig origin
# (`play.rs:558-566` falls back to `|world_scale|` when no collider is
# authored; `:768` is the `is_renderable` guard that lets it get there at
# all). Both are unauthored obstacles. The mesh stays in the repo and out of
# the scene until there is a collider that can express it.
#
# **And a collider is not the only thing it is waiting for.** Being in no
# scene means nothing renders it and no gate covers it, and that showed: its
# UVs ran ALONG the tube instead of around it — measured median face U span
# 0.4968 on an 8-sided tube where 0.125 is correct — from the first build
# until 2026-08-26, because `tube()` took U from `atan2` about Blender z and
# the bracing's axes are x and z. It is correct now by measurement, not
# because anyone looked at it. Whoever wires the collider should expect to be
# the first person to SEE this mesh, and should budget for finding more than
# one thing wrong with it.
#
# **`Drop` is not gated.** It is a `CharacterController` positioned to fall
# onto the deck so a human, or a future `green.sh` row, has a settle height to
# measure — this scene's `GOLDEN` row is an image compare with no `--sim`, so
# nothing here executes a tick or reads that height. Same story as
# `rig_deck.loom`'s `Drop`.
#
# **Camera** sits low off the north-west corner, adjusted from the brief's
# starting guess until all four zones actually read in frame: the deck
# surface, the bulwark above it, the piles below, and the seam where they
# cross the water. See the render notes in the task report for what it looks
# like at this framing and at eye height.
#
# **Task 4 adds the shed, its corrugated roof, the mast, and both bollards** —
# five nodes, textured, colliders transcribed from the primitives they
# replace. Positions and half-extents are copied verbatim from
# `build_shed.py`, `build_roof.py` and `build_hardware.py`'s own stdout,
# rerun immediately before authoring this file:
#
#     shed:     node pos=(-4.00, 2.60,  4.60) half_extents=(4.500, 1.200, 2.000)
#     roof:     node pos=(-4.00, 3.95,  4.60) half_extents=(4.900, 0.150, 2.400)
#     mast:     node pos=( 8.00, 3.60,  3.00) half_extents=(0.180, 2.200, 0.180)
#     bollard:  node pos=(-6.60, 1.70, -6.40) half_extents=(0.220, 0.300, 0.220)
#     bollard:  node pos=(-3.40, 1.70, -6.40) half_extents=(0.220, 0.300, 0.220)
#
# `BollardWest` and `BollardEast` are one mesh, two nodes (spec §1 Case 6) —
# the same `[[asset]]` referenced twice, not duplicated.
#
# **The shed's north face sits at world z = 2.600** (center z 4.60 minus its
# 2.000 z half-extent). That face is `Mirror`/`MirrorFrame`'s mount in
# `deeper_demo.loom`. Neither is rebuilt or added to this scene — spec §3
# leaves that zone untouched, and the frame guards a stack the demo records
# as having cost an hour to get right — but nothing on the SHED may ever
# project past that plane, in this scene or a later one, or the frame's flush
# mount breaks the next time someone touches it. `loom measure --node
# Rig/Shed` gives min z 2.5999999: flush, as built.
#
# **The ROOF does cross it, by 400 mm, and that is correct.** `loom measure
# --node Rig/ShedRoof` gives min z 2.1999998 — the eaves overhang, and
# `deeper_demo.loom`'s own `ShedRoof` primitive (pos z 4.60, scale z 2.40 →
# z 2.200..7.000) reproduced exactly rather than a new encroachment. It is
# harmless because it passes OVER the frame, not through it: the roof
# occupies y 3.800..4.094 and `MirrorFrame` tops out at y 3.660 (pos y 2.600,
# scale y 1.060), leaving 140 mm of clearance. Extend the rule to the roof
# and you forbid an overhang the primitive already had.

